#!/usr/bin/python3
"""hearth-recovery: the engine behind Hearth Recovery, the USB stick that repairs an installed Hearth OS
(like SteamOS's recovery image). Its screen (ui/shell.qml) runs these commands; every one prints JSON.

Run as root (the recovery USB's screen runs as root). Works on the drives of the PC the stick started:

  hearth-recovery scan                       the Hearth OS installs on the internal drives: device, edition, users,
                                             deployments (current first, then previous) with versions and dates
  hearth-recovery set-default SYS INDEX      start deployment INDEX next time (`ostree admin set-default`), the GRUB
                                             menu rewritten to match on Peppermint (hearth-bls-grub), read back
  hearth-recovery reset-display SYS [--desktop] [--user NAME]
                                             Game Mode's display settings back to automatic (renderer, resolution,
                                             display.sh...), and with --desktop the next start in Desktop Mode
  hearth-recovery send-logs SYS [NOTE]       the installed system's journal (last boots) and Hearth's own logs,
                                             sent the way hearth-send-logs sends them: {"code": ..., "url": ...}
  hearth-recovery update SYS                 the newest published image of that edition deployed onto the drive
                                             (ostree container image deploy), keeping /etc changes, /var and /home;
                                             every version already there is pinned while it runs, so none is removed
  hearth-recovery factory-check SYS          what a factory reset would erase: {"mode": "whole" | "hearth" | null, ...}
                                             whole: the drive has nothing else on it; hearth: only Hearth's own
                                             partitions (other systems on the drive stay); null: it can't be done safely
  hearth-recovery factory-reset SYS MODE     erases and installs Hearth OS again (hearth-install), only if
                                             factory-check still says MODE (what the person confirmed)
  hearth-recovery copy-targets [SYS]         drives files can be copied to: USB drives and other drives' data
                                             partitions (never SYS's own, the USB stick, or another system's)
  hearth-recovery copy-home SYS USER PART    copies USER's home folder onto partition PART (one of copy-targets)
  hearth-recovery wifi-list | wifi-connect SSID [PASSWORD] | net-status
  hearth-recovery restart [SYS] | poweroff   restart (into that Hearth OS when the firmware allows: BootNext)

SYS is a system's "id" from `scan` (its partition, and the btrfs subvolume holding the ostree sysroot).
Long commands print progress lines `stage: {json}` and end with `result: {json}` or `error: <message>`.

For tests, HEARTH_RECOVERY_FAKE=<dir> replaces the PC's drives with folders: <dir>/devices.json lists them
({"name": ..., "fstype": ..., "uuid": ..., "disk": ..., "parttype": ..., "partlabel": ..., "removable": ...}) and
<dir>/<name> is each one's contents; "mounting" is a symlink. HEARTH_RECOVERY_LOGS_URL replaces the log service,
HEARTH_RECOVERY_INSTALLER hearth-install.
"""
